WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:02.100
Colt: Hey everyone, me and some of my

00:02.100 --> 00:03.750
stock asset friends here

00:03.750 --> 00:05.700
are very happy to welcome you to the Git

00:05.700 --> 00:07.290
and GitHub bootcamp.

00:07.290 --> 00:09.540
So none of these people are my real friends,

00:09.540 --> 00:12.660
but they are free images from my slide software,

00:12.660 --> 00:13.800
which is pretty good too

00:13.800 --> 00:15.270
and they'll be making some appearances

00:15.270 --> 00:17.250
throughout the course in my diagrams.

00:17.250 --> 00:19.710
Anyway, none of these fine folks are actually me.

00:19.710 --> 00:20.640
This is me here.

00:20.640 --> 00:21.473
Hello everyone.

00:21.473 --> 00:22.631
My name is Colt Steele.

00:22.631 --> 00:25.650
I never know how much detail to go into in my background,

00:25.650 --> 00:28.740
but I'll say that I've been teaching people how to program

00:28.740 --> 00:29.880
for almost a decade.

00:29.880 --> 00:31.860
I've helped thousands of people change careers

00:31.860 --> 00:32.693
in my in-person bootcamps,

00:32.693 --> 00:35.940
but I've also reached over a million students online.

00:35.940 --> 00:37.980
Ugh, I always feel a bit weird talking about it,

00:37.980 --> 00:39.660
but you can always check out my LinkedIn

00:39.660 --> 00:42.600
or my instructor bio if you want more information.

00:42.600 --> 00:43.470
All that really matters

00:43.470 --> 00:46.020
is that I actually really enjoy teaching Git.

00:46.020 --> 00:47.460
It's a challenging thing to teach

00:47.460 --> 00:50.910
and it's a creative challenge for the teacher.

00:50.910 --> 00:52.260
So I hope you enjoy the course.

00:52.260 --> 00:54.300
I've spent a lot of time trying to make it

00:54.300 --> 00:56.250
the best possible Git course.

00:56.250 --> 00:58.350
So if you're watching this, you probably already know

00:58.350 --> 01:01.290
that Git is this amazing, universal,

01:01.290 --> 01:04.530
widespread tool that, along with GitHub, enables

01:04.530 --> 01:07.540
and empowers millions and millions of users

01:07.540 --> 01:10.650
and companies ranging from tiny startups all the way up

01:10.650 --> 01:13.260
to Fortune 50 companies, to build things together,

01:13.260 --> 01:16.680
to collaborate and to maintain applications.

01:16.680 --> 01:18.090
So you probably already know that

01:18.090 --> 01:19.350
and a lot of people know it.

01:19.350 --> 01:23.340
They know it's crucial, yet it often goes ignored.

01:23.340 --> 01:26.820
It's not the most glamorous topic to learn at the beginning.

01:26.820 --> 01:28.320
I totally get why a lot

01:28.320 --> 01:32.040
of people prioritize learning JavaScript or CSS

01:32.040 --> 01:35.580
or Python or pretty much anything over Git.

01:35.580 --> 01:39.450
But the truth is, this is a very annoying expression

01:39.450 --> 01:42.300
that the best time to learn Git is yesterday.

01:42.300 --> 01:43.732
Somebody told me something very similar,

01:43.732 --> 01:46.980
a neighbor actually, about how the best time

01:46.980 --> 01:49.320
to plant fruit trees is five years ago.

01:49.320 --> 01:50.370
When you hear something like that,

01:50.370 --> 01:54.120
it's natural to want to punch that person,

01:54.120 --> 01:56.700
strangle them, or maybe just roll your eyes.

01:56.700 --> 01:58.860
But there is some truth to this,

01:58.860 --> 02:01.350
but also there's another piece to that sentence.

02:01.350 --> 02:03.420
The second best time to learn Git is now

02:03.420 --> 02:05.890
the second best time to plant fruit trees is today.

02:05.890 --> 02:08.250
Probably the second best time to plant fruit trees,

02:08.250 --> 02:09.720
it was four years ago.

02:09.720 --> 02:12.090
Anyway, the point is that it's very important to try

02:12.090 --> 02:13.421
and learn Git as soon as possible

02:13.421 --> 02:16.410
along whatever journey you're on.

02:16.410 --> 02:19.470
The sooner you learn it, the better, because it's not just

02:19.470 --> 02:23.070
about learning some syntax that takes some time,

02:23.070 --> 02:25.800
but you know, you can watch a video or a course

02:25.800 --> 02:28.170
or read a book and and learn the basics,

02:28.170 --> 02:29.220
but it takes more work.

02:29.220 --> 02:31.950
There's more than just learning the basic syntax.

02:31.950 --> 02:34.140
The real work comes with part two

02:34.140 --> 02:35.760
or what I'm calling step two,

02:35.760 --> 02:39.060
actually living as a Git user and breathing Git.

02:39.060 --> 02:41.190
It needs to become second nature.

02:41.190 --> 02:44.220
Git becomes a fundamental part of your workflow.

02:44.220 --> 02:45.480
So that takes time, right?

02:45.480 --> 02:47.520
That's why the best time to learn Git is yesterday,

02:47.520 --> 02:48.614
blah, blah, blah.

02:48.614 --> 02:52.500
But also, that's the goal of this course, to help Git

02:52.500 --> 02:54.000
become second nature.

02:54.000 --> 02:56.010
So not just to teach you the basics,

02:56.010 --> 03:00.330
it's also helping you just learn and live Git,

03:00.330 --> 03:03.120
breathe Git in as a user and that takes time

03:03.120 --> 03:04.530
and it takes practice.

03:04.530 --> 03:06.150
So that's why this course is more

03:06.150 --> 03:08.670
than just a bunch of lectures, although it's a ton

03:08.670 --> 03:11.683
of lectures, obviously I can't get around that.

03:11.683 --> 03:14.700
That is one of the core pieces of the course.

03:14.700 --> 03:15.750
It has to be educational.

03:15.750 --> 03:18.630
There's going to be that explaining the syntax

03:18.630 --> 03:20.340
and demonstrating how things work.

03:20.340 --> 03:23.310
But also there are quite a few exercises that I built to try

03:23.310 --> 03:25.500
and force you to get practice, to try

03:25.500 --> 03:30.500
and sort of steer you towards becoming one with Git.

03:31.290 --> 03:33.360
So I highly recommend you do those.

03:33.360 --> 03:35.160
I'll point them out in a separate video.

03:35.160 --> 03:37.170
I wanna talk about some strategies for them.

03:37.170 --> 03:38.812
I also tried to make it somewhat interesting

03:38.812 --> 03:42.000
and entertaining, but also visually,

03:42.000 --> 03:45.210
I spent way too much time, I think, making diagrams

03:45.210 --> 03:48.240
and Git is something that I think benefits a lot

03:48.240 --> 03:50.640
from thinking about in a visual way.

03:50.640 --> 03:52.560
So I spent a lot of time making these diagrams.

03:52.560 --> 03:53.790
I hope you appreciate them.

03:53.790 --> 03:56.370
And I guess that's really all I have to say right now.

03:56.370 --> 03:57.570
Welcome to the course, everyone.

03:57.570 --> 03:58.980
I hope you enjoy it.

03:58.980 --> 04:00.540
And I'll end this welcome video

04:00.540 --> 04:04.020
with one not-at-all important note.

04:04.020 --> 04:07.320
In my courses, I often reference my pet chickens

04:07.320 --> 04:08.160
throughout my courses.

04:08.160 --> 04:11.640
I'll use their names in different files or in exercises,

04:11.640 --> 04:14.820
in diagrams, and students inevitably demand photos

04:14.820 --> 04:16.050
or videos of my chickens.

04:16.050 --> 04:17.529
So I'm just gonna get it out of the way here.

04:17.529 --> 04:20.370
If you wanna see them, here they are.

04:20.370 --> 04:22.350
All right, so we'll go ahead and get started.

04:22.350 --> 04:24.950
Once again, welcome and I hope you enjoy the course.
